# IMPLEMENTATION: SINGLE FILE INPUT → AUTOMATIC FOUR-ZONE ENERGY ANALYSIS

Repository: https://github.com/likhithvu13-afk/community-project

I want to implement a completely automated data processing system in my existing Sustainable Energy Digital Twin prototype.

IMPORTANT: There must be ONLY ONE USER INPUT: FILE UPLOAD.

The user should not have to:
- Enter zone names.
- Enter threshold values.
- Map columns manually.
- Enter energy readings.
- Select individual zones.
- Configure analysis parameters.

Everything must be handled automatically by the backend.

## 1. SINGLE FILE UPLOAD

Provide one file-upload component.

Accept CSV, Excel and JSON files.

The uploaded file may contain energy readings for multiple zones in one dataset.

## 2. AUTOMATIC DATA PROCESSING

When a file is uploaded:

1. Detect the file format.
2. Extract the data.
3. Identify column names and their meanings.
4. Standardize the data into a predefined internal schema.
5. Identify timestamps, power readings, asset identifiers and zone information.
6. Clean missing and duplicate records.
7. Automatically classify records into the existing four zones.

Use intelligent column-name matching and asset identification.

If zone identification is ambiguous, flag the affected records instead of silently assigning them to an incorrect zone.

Do not ask the user to configure the data.

## 3. FOUR FIXED BACKEND ZONES

Use the existing zone definitions in the repository.

- HVAC Unit 01 — hvac-unit-01
- Lighting Zone 3B — lighting-zone-3b
- Computer Cluster 1 — computer-cluster-1
- Printer Zone 1 — printer-zone-1

Maintain four fixed zone configurations in the backend.

## 4. PREDEFINED THRESHOLD ENGINE

Create a centralized backend configuration containing fixed thresholds for each zone.

Each zone must have its own:
- Normal operating power range.
- Warning threshold.
- Critical threshold.
- Baseline power.
- Energy consumption limits.

Determine sensible initial thresholds using existing project configuration, sample sensor readings and zone-specific operating characteristics.

Do not use the same threshold for every zone.

Thresholds must be stored in backend configuration, not entered by the user.

Make threshold values configurable by developers without requiring frontend changes.

Use appropriate units and time intervals.

## 5. AUTOMATIC ZONE-WISE CLASSIFICATION

After processing, compare each reading with the predefined threshold for its corresponding zone.

Classify each record:

- NORMAL: Reading is within the expected operating range.
- WARNING: Reading exceeds the warning threshold.
- CRITICAL: Reading exceeds the critical threshold.

Use the existing project's logic where possible.

Avoid generating false alerts from isolated readings. Consider appropriate rolling averages or consecutive readings when sufficient timestamp data is available.

## 6. AUTOMATIC TABLE GENERATION

Generate a standardized processed table automatically.

Expected columns:

timestamp | asset_id | zone_name | power_w | threshold_w | excess_power_w | energy_kwh | carbon_kg | status

The table must be generated from the uploaded dataset.

Do not hardcode sample readings.

## 7. AUTOMATIC ANALYSIS

For all four zones calculate:

- Total energy consumption.
- Average power.
- Peak power.
- Baseline consumption.
- Excess power.
- Energy wastage.
- Carbon emissions.
- Number of warning readings.
- Number of critical readings.
- Overall zone status.

Calculate energy using actual time intervals wherever possible.

Do not confuse instantaneous power (W) with energy (kWh).

Use the project's configured electricity tariff and carbon emission factor.

## 8. AUTOMATIC RESULT GENERATION

Once processing finishes, automatically display:

A. Overall summary:
- Total energy consumed.
- Total estimated energy wasted.
- Total carbon emissions.
- Overall system status.

B. Four-zone analysis table:
- Zone name.
- Average power.
- Peak power.
- Threshold.
- Energy consumed.
- Excess consumption.
- Carbon emissions.
- Status.

C. Visualizations:
- Zone-wise consumption comparison.
- Actual power versus threshold.
- Energy wastage comparison.
- Carbon emissions by zone.

D. AI recommendations:
Generate recommendations based on actual threshold violations and energy readings.

Explain which zone is inefficient, why it is inefficient and what action can reduce consumption.

## 9. DYNAMIC DATA INTEGRATION

The uploaded dataset must become the active data source.

All results must be calculated from that dataset.

Integrate the processing pipeline into the existing Digital Twin dashboard.

Preserve existing sample-data functionality and other existing features.

Do not build a separate application.

## 10. IMPLEMENTATION REQUIREMENTS

Inspect the existing code before modifying it.

Reuse existing functions in:
- core/input.py
- core/energy.py
- core/twin.py
- core/recommender.py
- core/config.py

Create additional modules only if needed.

Implement the complete backend and frontend integration.

Test the workflow using one uploaded file containing readings from all four zones.

Verify:
- Correct data extraction.
- Correct zone identification.
- Correct threshold application.
- Correct energy calculations.
- Correct status classification.
- Correct generated tables and charts.

FINAL EXPECTATION:

ONE FILE UPLOAD → AUTOMATIC PROCESSING → FOUR FIXED ZONE CLASSIFICATION → PREDEFINED THRESHOLD COMPARISON → AUTOMATIC ANALYSIS → DYNAMIC RESULTS.

No additional user input or manual configuration should be necessary.

Implement and test the actual working feature, not just a visual prototype.


